'''Doncaster North''' is a [[Constituencies of the Parliament of the United Kingdom|constituency]]{{#tag:ref|A [[county constituency]] (for the purposes of election expenses and type of returning officer)|group= n}} in [[South Yorkshire]], England, represented in the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom|UK Parliament]] since 2005 by [[Ed Miliband]] of the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour Party]]. From 2010 until 2015, he was [[Leader of the Opposition (United Kingdom)|Leader of the Opposition]] before he lost the [[2015 United Kingdom general election|2015 general election]] to [[David Cameron]] and the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservatives]].{{#tag:ref|As with all constituencies, the constituency elects one Member of Parliament (MP) by the [[First-past-the-post voting|first past the post]] system of election at least every five years.|group= n}} Part of the [[Red wall (British politics)|red wall]], it was formerly a Labour stronghold, until the [[2019 United Kingdom general election|2019 general election]] when it became a Labour-Conservative marginal.

== History ==
The constituency was created in [[1983 United Kingdom general election|1983]] from parts of the former constituencies of [[Don Valley (UK Parliament constituency)|Don Valley]], [[Doncaster (UK Parliament constituency)|Doncaster]], [[Goole (UK Parliament constituency)|Goole]], and was a [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] [[safe seat|stronghold]] until the [[2019 United Kingdom general election|2019 general election]], when Labour held the seat with a significantly reduced majority of 2,370 votes. [[Ed Miliband]] has served as the MP for the constituency since [[2005 United Kingdom general election|2005]], and he also served as [[Leader of the Labour Party (UK)|Leader of the Labour Party]] and [[Leader of the Opposition (United Kingdom)|Leader of the Opposition]] from [[2010 Labour Party leadership election (UK)|2010]] until stepping down after the [[2015 United Kingdom general election|2015 general election]].

== Boundaries ==
{{maplink|frame=yes|frame-align=right|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/Doncaster North (UK Parliament constituency) 2010}}|frame-height=200|frame-width=250|text=Map of boundaries 2010–2024}}
'''1983–1997''': The Metropolitan Borough of Doncaster wards of Adwick, Askern, Bentley Central, Bentley North Road, Hatfield, Stainforth, and Thorne.

'''1997–2010''': The Metropolitan Borough of Doncaster wards of Adwick, Askern, Bentley Central, Bentley North Road, Stainforth, and Thorne.

'''2010–2024''': The Metropolitan Borough of Doncaster wards of Adwick, Askern Spa, Bentley, Great North Road, Mexborough, Sprotbrough, Stainforth and Moorends.

'''2024–present''': The City of Doncaster wards of Adwick le Street & Carcroft, Bentley, Mexborough, Norton & Askern, Roman Ridge, Sprotbrough, and Stainforth & Barnby Dun.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2023/1230/schedules/made |at=Schedule 1 Part 9 Yorkshire and the Humber region}}</ref>
:''Minor changes to reflect modifications to ward boundaries and names.''

== Constituency profile ==
The constituency covers largely rural areas north and west of [[Doncaster]], and suburban areas too, stretching to partly suburban [[Mexborough]] in the west, [[Bentley, South Yorkshire|Bentley]] that lies on the northern banks of the [[River Don, Yorkshire|River Don]] and [[Adwick le Street|Adwick]] which is farther north. The seat is an area with moderate typical incomes which has yet to fully recover from the almost total end of the local coal mining industry;{{#tag:ref|See [[British Coal]]|group= n}} however, large numbers of constituents now work in construction and manufacturing.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://neighbourhood.statistics.gov.uk/|title=Check Browser Settings|work=statistics.gov.uk|access-date=1 January 2013|archive-date=11 February 2003|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20030211201309/http://www.neighbourhood.statistics.gov.uk/|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="auto">{{Rayment-hc|d|2|date=March 2012}}</ref><ref>[https://www.theguardian.com/politics/constituency/876/doncaster-north Guardian Unlimited Politics] Election results from 1992 to the present</ref><ref>[http://www.politicsresources.net/area/uk.htm Politics Resources] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150626151923/http://www.politicsresources.net/area/uk.htm |date=26 June 2015 }} Election results from 1945 to present</ref> Alongside these typically Labour-supporting areas, the constituency also contains more Conservative-leaning areas such as [[Sprotbrough]], council ward of the Conservative's next Parliamentary candidate, Glenn Bluff.

Doncaster North has returned Labour MPs since [[1983 United Kingdom general election|1983]], when the constituency was created. The preceding constituencies had returned Labour MPs at every general election since at least [[1935 United Kingdom general election|1935]].

At the [[2016 United Kingdom European Union membership referendum|EU referendum held on 23 June 2016]], Doncaster voted to leave the [[European Union]]. This was a contrast to the views of the constituency's MP [[Ed Miliband]], who advocated a "Remain" vote. Miliband was re-elected on 12 December with a significantly reduced majority of 2,370 votes at the 2019 general election, which is attributed to [[Reform UK|Brexit Party]] candidate Andy Stewart picking up 8,294 votes and finishing in third place with 20.4% of the vote.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.doncasterfreepress.co.uk/news/politics/uk-backs-leaving-eu-as-doncaster-votes-two-to-one-to-get-out-1-7980707|title=UK backs leaving EU as Doncaster votes two to one to get out|work=Doncaster Free Press}}</ref> Following the collapse in the Labour vote, the seat is now marginal between Labour and the Conservatives.

