'''Wetherby and Easingwold''' is a proposed [[List of UK Parliament constituencies|constituency]] of the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] in the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom|UK Parliament]], partly in [[North Yorkshire]] and partly in [[West Yorkshire]]. Further to the completion of the [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]], it will first be contested at the [[2024 United Kingdom general election|2024 general election]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Yorkshire and the Humber |url=https://boundarycommissionforengland.independent.gov.uk/2023-review/yorkshire-and-the-humber/ |access-date=20 June 2023 |website=[[Boundary Commission for England]]}}</ref>
The Conservative MP for [[Elmet and Rothwell (UK Parliament constituency)|Elmet and Rothwell]], [[Alec Shelbrooke]], has been selected for the constituency.<ref>{{cite web |last=Greenwood |first=Darren |date=6 April 2023 |title=Tories choose Alec Shelbrooke MP to stand in Wetherby and Easingwold |url=https://www.yorkpress.co.uk/news/23440255.tories-choose-alec-shelbrooke-mp-stand-wetherby-easingwold/ |access-date=30 July 2023 |website=York Press }}</ref>
== Boundaries == The constituency will be composed of the following wards (as they existed on 1 December 2020):
* The District of Hambleton wards of: Easingwold; Huby; Raskelf & White Horse. * The Borough of Harrogate wards of: Bishop Monkton & Newby; Boroughbridge; Marston Moor; Ouseburn; Spofforth with Lower Wharfedale. * The City of Leeds wards of: Harewood; Wetherby. * The District of Selby wards of: Appleton Roebuck & Church Fenton; Tadcaster.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=The Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2023/1230/schedules/made |at=Schedule 1 Part 9 Yorkshire and the Humber region}}</ref>
It will comprise the following areas:<ref>{{cite web |title=New Seat Details – Wetherby and Easingwold |url=https://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/fcgi-bin/calcwork23.py?seat=Wetherby%20and%20Easingwold |access-date=30 July 2023 |website=www.electoralcalculus.co.uk}}</ref>
From the former [[Hambleton District]] in North Yorkshire:
* [[Easingwold]], [[Huby, Hambleton|Huby]], [[Raskelf]] and [[Kilburn White Horse|White Horse]] from [[Thirsk and Malton (UK Parliament constituency)|Thirsk and Malton]]
From the former [[Selby District]] in North Yorkshire:
* [[Appleton Roebuck]], [[Church Fenton]] and [[Tadcaster]] from [[Selby and Ainsty (UK Parliament constituency)|Selby and Ainsty]]
From the former [[Borough of Harrogate]] in North Yorkshire:
* [[Boroughbridge]] from [[Harrogate and Knaresborough (UK Parliament constituency)|Harrogate and Knaresborough]] * [[Marston Moor]] and [[Spofforth, North Yorkshire|Spofforth]] with Lower [[Wharfedale]] from [[Selby and Ainsty (UK Parliament constituency)|Selby and Ainsty]] * [[Bishop Monkton]] and [[Newby with Mulwith|Newby]] from [[Skipton and Ripon (UK Parliament constituency)|Skipton and Ripon]]
From the [[City of Leeds]] in West Yorkshire:
* [[Harewood (ward)|Harewood]] and [[Wetherby (ward)|Wetherby]] from [[Elmet and Rothwell (UK Parliament constituency)|Elmet and Rothwell]] With effect from 1 April 2023, the second tier authorities in the county of North Yorkshire were abolished and absorbed into the new unitary authority of North Yorkshire.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The North Yorkshire (Structural Changes) Order 2022 |url=https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2022/328/introduction/made}}</ref> The constituency will therefore now comprise the following from the 2024 general election:
* The City of Leeds wards of: Harewood; Wetherby. * The North Yorkshire electoral divisions of: Appleton Roebuck & Church Fenton; Boroughbridge & Claro (part); Easingwold; Hillside & Raskelf (part); Huby & Tollerton; Ouseburn; Spofforth with Lower Wharfedale & Tockwith; Tadcaster; Wathvale & Bishop Monkton (part).<ref>{{Cite web |title=New Seat Details - Wetherby and Easingwold |url=https://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/fcgi-bin/calcwork23.py?seat=Wetherby+and+Easingwold |access-date=2024-04-15 |website=www.electoralcalculus.co.uk}}</ref>
