'''Beverley and Holderness''' is a [[Constituencies of the Parliament of the United Kingdom|county constituency]] in the [[East Riding of Yorkshire]] for the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom]]. It elects one [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|Member of Parliament]] (MP) at least once every five years by the [[first-past-the-post]] electoral system. The constituency has been represented by [[Graham Stuart (politician)|Graham Stuart]] of the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative Party]] since the [[2005 United Kingdom general election|2005 general election]].

==Constituency profile==
The constituency covers the southeastern portion of the [[East Riding of Yorkshire]] and borders [[East Yorkshire (UK Parliament constituency)|East Yorkshire]], [[Haltemprice and Howden (UK Parliament constituency)|Haltemprice and Howden]], [[Kingston upon Hull North (UK Parliament constituency)|Kingston upon Hull North]] and [[Kingston upon Hull East (UK Parliament constituency)|Kingston upon Hull East]] seats. It also borders a stretch of the [[North Sea]] coast from [[Skipsea]] to [[Spurn]] Point, and the north bank of the [[Humber]] Estuary inland to [[Hedon]].{{citation needed|date=November 2019}}

From and including the [[2010 United Kingdom general election|2010 general election]] the composition of the seat has changed; the civil parishes [[Brandesburton]] and [[Woodmansey]] were transferred to other seats (East Yorkshire and Haltemprice and Howden respectively). [[Middleton on the Wolds]] and [[Newbald]] were gained from the same respective seats.<ref name="EYN">{{cite magazine |date=April 2010 |title=Are you ready to vote in the next election? |magazine=East Riding News |publisher=East Riding of Yorkshire Council |page=3}}</ref>

Besides Beverley, the seat incorporates the market town of [[Hedon]], which was itself a parliamentary borough until [[Hedon (UK Parliament constituency)|that seat]] was abolished at the [[Reform Act 1832]].{{citation needed|date=November 2019}}

==History==
The seat has been won by the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]] candidate since its creation in 1997, on a majority ranging between 1.7% of the votes cast in the [[2001 United Kingdom general election|2001 general election]] and 38.2% in the [[2019 United Kingdom general election|2019 general election]]. The party of the runner-up candidate has been [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] six times and [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrat]] once, as of the 2019 general election.{{citation needed|date=July 2024}}

==Boundaries==
'''1997–2010''': The East Yorkshire Borough of Beverley [[Ward (electoral subdivision)|wards]] of Cherry Holme, Leconfield, Leven, Minster North, Minster South, Molescroft, St Mary's East, St Mary's West, Tickton, Walkington, and Woodmansey, and the Borough of Holderness.<ref>{{UK-LEG |title=Parliamentary Constituencies (England) Order 1995 |path=uksi/1995/1626/schedules/made |asmade=yes |access_date=26 November 2019}}</ref>

'''2010–2024''': The District of East Riding of Yorkshire wards of Beverley Rural, Mid Holderness, Minster and Woodmansey, North Holderness, St Mary's, South East Holderness, and South West Holderness.<ref>{{UK-LEG |title= Parliamentary Constituencies (England) Order 2007 |path=uksi/2007/1681/schedules/made |asmade=yes |access_date=26 November 2019}}</ref>

'''2024–present''': The District of East Riding of Yorkshire wards of: Beverley Rural, Mid Holderness, Minster and Woodmansey, St Mary’s, South East Holderness, and South West Holderness.<ref>{{UK-LEG|title=Parliamentary Constituencies Order 2023 |path=uksi/2023/1230/schedules/made|asmade=yes |accessdate=20 November 2023}}</ref>

