'''Harrogate and Knaresborough''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|h|æ|r|ə|g|ə|t|...|ˈ|n|ɛər|z|b|ər|ə|,_|-|ɡ|eɪ|t|_|-|,_|-|ɡ|ɪ|t|_|-}}){{refn|{{Cite EPD|18|Harrogate|page=225}}}}{{refn|{{Dictionary.com|access-date=22 January 2016|Harrogate}}}} is a [[United Kingdom constituencies|parliamentary constituency]] in [[North Yorkshire]] which has been represented in the [[House of Commons of the United Kingdom|House of Commons]] of the [[Parliament of the United Kingdom|UK Parliament]] since 2024 by [[Tom Gordon (British politician)|Tom Gordon]], an [[Member of Parliament (United Kingdom)|MP]] from the [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrats]]. The constituency was formed in the 1997 boundary changes, before which it was named [[Harrogate (UK Parliament constituency)|Harrogate]].

==Constituency profile==
An area with little [[unemployment]], a relatively large [[Retirement|retired]] population and large [[neighbourhood]]s of high house prices{{#tag:ref|In the 2001 census: worklessness was the status of (see Harrogate 009 Middle Layer SOA for access to the whole district): 1.0% of working age people compared to Yorkshire and the Humber: 2.6% England 2.3%<br />However, in the [http://neighbourhood.statistics.gov.uk 2001 Census publication] "Indices of Deprivation and Classification: Social Grade" 0.27% of the wider District population of 69,614 of working age were Class E: On state benefit, unemployed, lowest grade workers, slightly higher than 0.22% Yorkshire and the Humber average and 0.24% national average|group= n}}<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.mouseprice.com/property-for-sale/harrogate+north+yorkshire|title=Property for Sale in Harrogate, North Yorkshire - Mouseprice|website=www.mouseprice.com}}</ref> the former [[Harrogate (UK Parliament constituency)|Harrogate]] constituency was a safe [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative]] seat.  When former Chancellor [[Norman Lamont]] stood for the Harrogate and Knaresborough seat in the [[Labour Party (UK)|Labour]] [[Landslide victory|landslide]] general election in [[1997 United Kingdom general election|1997]], Harrogate moved the way of other [[spa town]]s in England such as [[Bath, Somerset|Bath]], and more urban and less touristic [[Cheltenham]], by returning a non-Conservative candidate. The [[Liberal Democrats (UK)|Liberal Democrat]] MP [[Phil Willis]] was elected, and served until [[Andrew Jones (British politician)|Andrew Jones]] regained the seat for [[Conservative Party (UK)|his party]] on Willis's retirement in the [[2010 United Kingdom general election|2010 general election]] with a swing of 9.1% and a margin of 1,039 votes.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.yorkshirepost.co.uk/election/HARROGATE-AND-KNARESBOROUGH-Blow-to.6284574.jp|title=Harrogate and Knaresborough: Blow to Lib Dems as close-run race ends in loss|date=10 May 2010|work=Yorkshire Post|publisher=Johnston Press Digital Publishing|access-date=15 May 2010}}</ref>

==Boundaries==
{{maplink|raw={{Wikipedia:Map data/Harrogate and Knaresborough (UK Parliament constituency) 2010}}|frame=yes|frame-align=left|frame-width=220|frame-height=220|text=Map of 2010–2024 boundaries}}
'''1997–2010''': The Borough of Harrogate wards of Bilton, Duchy, East Central, Granby, Harlow, Knaresborough East, Knaresborough West, New Park, Pannal, Starbeck, Wedderburn, and West Central.

'''2010–2024''': The Borough of Harrogate wards of [[Bilton-in-Ainsty|Bilton]], [[Boroughbridge]], Claro, Granby, Harlow Moor, High Harrogate, Hookstone, [[Killinghall]], Knaresborough East, Knaresborough King James, Knaresborough Scriven Park, Low Harrogate, New Park, Pannal, Rossett, Saltergate, Starbeck, Stray, and Woodfield.

'''2024–present''': Bilton & Nidd Gorge; Bilton Grange & New Park; Boroughbridge & Claro (majority); Coppice Valley & Duchy; Fairfax & Starbeck; Harlow & St Georges; High Harrogate & Kingsley; Killinghall, Hampsthwaite & Saltergate; Knaresborough East; Knaresborough West; Oatlands & Pannal; Stray, Woodlands & Hookstone; Valley Gardens & Central Harrogate.<ref>{{Cite web |title=New Seat Details - Harrogate and Knaresborough |url=https://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/fcgi-bin/calcwork23.py?seat=Harrogate+and+Knaresborough |access-date=2024-04-15 |website=www.electoralcalculus.co.uk}}</ref>

Boundary changes, proposed in [[2023 Periodic Review of Westminster constituencies]] described as following: ''Minor changes to align with revised ward boundaries.''

As its name suggests, the constituency is centred on the towns of [[Harrogate]] and [[Knaresborough]], with no parts more than {{convert|10|mi}} away from either.

==History==
Before 1950 the two eponymous towns had been part of the [[Ripon (UK Parliament constituency)|Ripon constituency]].  The constituency was created as [[Harrogate (UK Parliament constituency)|Harrogate]] and following boundary changes in 1997 the name was changed to 'Harrogate and Knaresborough'.

The current constituency embraces three former [[borough constituency|borough constituencies]]: [[Aldborough (UK Parliament constituency)|Aldborough]] (now a suburb of [[Boroughbridge]] civil parish) and [[Boroughbridge (UK Parliament constituency)|Boroughbridge]], which were abolished as [[rotten borough|'rotten boroughs']] by the [[Great Reform Act, 1832]], and [[Knaresborough (UK Parliament constituency)|Knaresborough]], abolished in 1885.

